@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Markdown Documentation";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-1">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Sidebar Navigation -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- Reading Content Area -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm mb-4">
                <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

                    <h2 class="mb-3">Markdown Documentation</h2>

                    <p class="lead">
                        <strong>Markdown</strong> is a lightweight markup
                        language that formats plain text using simple,
                        readable symbols. GitHub automatically renders
                        Markdown files with headings, lists, links, tables,
                        and code blocks.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Markdown files commonly use the <code>.md</code>
                        extension. The most important example in many
                        repositories is <code>README.md</code>, which
                        introduces the project and explains how to use it.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-info">
                        <strong>Remember:</strong> Markdown is plain text.
                        You can edit it using Visual Studio Code, Notepad,
                        or GitHub's built-in file editor.
                    </div>

                    <hr />

                    <h4>1. Headings</h4>

                    <p>
                        Use the hash symbol (<code>#</code>) followed by a
                        space to create headings. The number of hashes
                        determines the heading level.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code># Document Title
## Major Section
### Subsection
#### Smaller Heading
##### Level Five Heading
###### Level Six Heading</code></pre>

                    <h5>Rendered Example</h5>

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
                        <h2>Document Title</h2>
                        <h4>Major Section</h4>
                        <h5>Subsection</h5>
                    </div>

                    <hr />

                    <h4>2. Text Formatting</h4>

                    <p>Use Markdown symbols to emphasize important text.</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>*Italic text*
**Bold text**
***Bold and italic text***
~~Strikethrough text~~</code></pre>

                    <h5>Rendered Example</h5>

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
                        <p><em>Italic text</em></p>
                        <p><strong>Bold text</strong></p>
                        <p><strong><em>Bold and italic text</em></strong></p>
                        <p><del>Strikethrough text</del></p>
                    </div>

                    <hr />

                    <h4>3. Lists</h4>

                    <p>Use hyphens or asterisks for bullet lists and numbers for ordered lists.</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>- First item
- Second item
  - Nested item





1. First step
2. Second step
3. Third step</code></pre>

                            <h5>Rendered Example</h5>

                                <div class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
                                <ul>
                                    <li>First item</li>
                                <li>
                            Second item
                        <ul>
                                    <li>Nested item</li>
                        </ul>
                            </li>
                            </ul>

                        <ol>
                    <li>First step</li>
                            <li>Second step</li>
                    <li>Third step</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>

                    <hr />

                        <h4>4. Links and Images</h4>

                    <p>
                        Links use square brackets for the display text and
                    parentheses for the destination URL. Images use the
                        same syntax with an exclamation mark at the beginning.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>[GitHub](https://github.com)

![Project Architecture](./images/architecture.png)</code></pre>

                            <h5>Rendered Example</h5>

                            <div class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
                        <p>
                        <a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                            GitHub
                            </a>
                        </p>
                    <p class="text-muted mb-0">
                            An image will appear when the specified image path
                    exists in the repository.
                        </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="alert alert-warning">
                    <strong>Tip:</strong> Use descriptive alternative text
                        for images. Relative paths such as
                    <code>./images/architecture.png</code> are useful when
                        the image is stored inside your repository.
                    </div>

                    <hr />

                        <h4>5. Inline Code and Code Blocks</h4>

                        <p>
                    Use single backticks for commands inside a sentence.
                        Use triple backticks for multi-line code examples.
                    Adding a language identifier enables syntax highlighting
                        on supported Markdown platforms.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>Use `git status` to check repository changes.</code></pre>





                    <p>Example of a fenced Bash code block:</p>

                        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>```bash
git checkout -b feature/login
git add .
git commit -m "Add login feature"
```</code></pre>

                    <p>
                    On GitHub, the fenced example is rendered as a formatted
                        code block rather than ordinary paragraph text.
                    </p>





                    <hr />

                    <h4>6. Tables</h4>

                            <p>Tables help present commands, descriptions, and other structured information.</p>

                                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `git pull` | Fetches and integrates remote changes |
| `git push` | Uploads local commits to a remote repository |
| `git status` | Shows the working tree status |</code></pre>

                                    <h5>Rendered Example</h5>

                                <div class="table-responsive">
                                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">
                                    <thead class="table-dark">
                                    <tr>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                    </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                <tbody>
                            <tr>
                        <td><code>git pull</code></td>
                    <td>Fetches and integrates remote changes</td>
                                </tr>
                    <tr>
                                    <td><code>git push</code></td>
                    <td>Uploads local commits to a remote repository</td>
                                </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>git status</code></td>
                        <td>Shows the working tree status</td>
                    </tr>
                            </tbody>
                    </table>
                    </div>


                    <hr />

                    <h4>7. Task Lists</h4>

                        <p>
                        GitHub supports task lists that are useful for tracking
                    project setup steps and documentation work.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>- [x] Create project repository
- [ ] Configure GitHub Actions
- [ ] Deploy production files</code></pre>

                        <h5>Rendered Example</h5>

                    <div class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
                        <p><input type="checkbox" checked disabled /> Create project repository</p>
                    <p><input type="checkbox" disabled /> Configure GitHub Actions</p>
                        <p class="mb-0"><input type="checkbox" disabled /> Deploy production files</p>
                    </div>


















                    <hr />

                    <h4>8. Writing a Good README.md</h4>

                        <p>
                        A useful README helps developers understand your project
                        before they start using or contributing to it.
                        </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code># Project Name

## Overview
Describe what your project does.

## Features
- Feature one
- Feature two

## Requirements
List the required software and dependencies.

## Installation
Explain how to clone and configure the project.

## Usage
Show how to run and use the application.

## Contributing
Explain how others can contribute.</code></pre>

                    <p>A README commonly includes:</p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>Project name and purpose</li>
                            <li>Features and screenshots</li>
                            <li>Prerequisites and installation steps</li>
                        <li>Usage instructions and examples</li>
                        <li>Contribution guidelines and license information</li>
                        </ul>

                            <hr />

                    <h4>9. Markdown Best Practices</h4>

                <ul>
            <li>Use headings to divide long documents into clear sections.</li>
        <li>Keep paragraphs short and easy to scan.</li>
    <li>Format terminal commands using inline code or fenced code blocks.</li>
<li>Use meaningful link text and image alternative text.</li>
                        <li>Include installation and usage instructions in your README.</li>
                        <li>Preview the rendered Markdown before publishing changes.</li>
</ul>

<div class="alert alert-success mt-4">
<h5 class="alert-heading">Key Takeaway</h5>
Markdown makes project documentation readable,
maintainable, and easy to publish. Learn these basic
elements to create better README files, contribution
guides, and technical documentation.
</div>

